    <DataNode elementId="LIPOIC-ACID" textLabel="(R)-lipoate" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="AGBQKNBQESQNJD-SSDOTTSWSA-M" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="500" centerY="210" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="xue, PMN, nilo-poyanco, SRI, caspi">|FRAME: LIPOIC-ACID &quot;Lipoate&quot;| is an organosulfur compound that is used as an essential cofactor by several important enzyme complexes. The functional group of lipoate is a ditholane ring, which is a cyclic disulfide. The enzyme complexes that require lipoate have a dedicated lipoyl domain, and lipoate is actually produced on this domain. The precursor |FRAME: CPD-195| molecule is first attached to a specific |FRAME: LYS| residue within the lipoyl domain, and is then converted to lipoate by |FRAME: EC-2.8.1.8|, which is also the source of the two sulfur atoms. Lipoate can be detached from the enzyme complex by |FRAME: EC-3.5.1.138|, and be re-attached to the complex by |FRAME: EC-6.3.1.20|. Enzyme known to require lipate include |FRAME: EC-1.2.1.105|, |FRAME: EC-1.2.1.104|, |FRAME: EC-1.4.1.27|, |FRAME: EC-1.2.1.25|, and |FRAME: EC-2.3.1.190|.</Comment>
